What I need from you to calculate CAC and LTV precisely
- Period(s) you want to measure (monthly / quarterly / trailing 12 months)
- Sales & marketing spend for the period (total S&M spend = marketing + sales)
- Number of new customers acquired in that same period
- Total active customers (or MRR customer count) and/or average revenue per user (ARPU)
- Churn rate (monthly or annual) or cohort retention curve
- Gross margin (contribution margin; revenue minus direct cost of goods sold)
If you don’t have all of these, I can run sensitivity scenarios.

Key formulas (simple, standard)
- CAC = (Total Sales & Marketing spend over period) / (Number of new customers acquired in same period)
- ARPU (monthly) = (Monthly revenue) / (Number of customers)
- Customer lifetime (months) ≈ 1 / (monthly churn rate)
- LTV (simple) = ARPU (monthly) × gross margin % × customer lifetime (months)
- LTV : CAC ratio = LTV / CAC
- CAC payback (months) = CAC / (ARPU × gross margin %)

Practical targets (rule of thumb)
- LTV:CAC > 3 : 1 is healthy for many SaaS/recurring businesses.
- CAC payback < 12 months is strong for high-growth companies; <18 months acceptable for enterprise.
- Gross margin matters: higher gross margin increases LTV proportionally.

Quick worked examples using your revenue figure (I’ll show three scenarios using reasonable assumptions so you can see sensitivity)

Facts we know:
- Revenue last quarter = $3.2M → monthly revenue ≈ $1,066,667

Example assumptions (you can swap these with real inputs):
Scenario A — Base case
- Customers = 1,000 → ARPU = $1,066.67/month
- Monthly churn = 2% → lifetime ≈ 50 months
- Gross margin = 75%
- S&M spend = $200K/month
- New customers = 50/month

Calculations:
- CAC = $200,000 / 50 = $4,000
- LTV = 1,066.67 × 0.75 × 50 ≈ $40,000
- LTV:CAC ≈ 40,000 / 4,000 = 10 : 1 (very strong)
- CAC payback = 4,000 / (1,066.67 × 0.75) ≈ 5 months

Interpretation: Very efficient unit economics — you recover CAC quickly and get a large LTV.

Scenario B — More churn / higher CAC
- Customers = 1,000 → ARPU = $1,066.67
- Monthly churn = 5% → lifetime = 20 months
- Gross margin = 70%
- S&M spend = $300K/month
- New customers = 50/month

Calculations:
- CAC = 300,000 / 50 = $6,000
- LTV = 1,066.67 × 0.70 × 20 ≈ $14,933
- LTV:CAC ≈ 14,933 / 6,000 ≈ 2.49 : 1
- CAC payback = 6,000 / (1,066.67 × 0.70) ≈ 8 months

Interpretation: Borderline LTV:CAC (<3). Need to reduce CAC, increase retention, or improve margin.

Scenario C — Lower ARPU / high churn (path to fix)
- Customers = 2,000 → ARPU = 533.33
- Monthly churn = 4% → lifetime = 25 months
- Gross margin = 65%
- S&M spend = 200K/month
- New customers = 100/month

Calculations:
- CAC = 200,000 / 100 = $2,000
- LTV = 533.33 × 0.65 × 25 ≈ $8,666
- LTV:CAC ≈ 8,666 / 2,000 ≈ 4.33 : 1
- CAC payback = 2,000 / (533.33 × 0.65) ≈ 5.8 months

Interpretation: Lower ARPU but cheaper acquisition and decent retention can still yield healthy economics.

