I can — but first a couple of housekeeping items and a short list of data I need so the numbers are correct.

1) Quick inconsistency to confirm
- You listed: cash = $2.1M, runway = 20 months, burn = $240K/month. Those three can’t all be true at face value because runway = cash / net burn. 2.1M / 240K = 8.75 months, not 20. Either:
  - The $240K/month is gross cash burn but you are offsetting by net cash inflows (so net burn is ~105K/month), or
  - Runway assumes cuts or a future plan (reducing burn), or
  - One of the numbers is misstated.
Please confirm how runway is calculated (i.e., what “burn” you used, or whether runway is projected after changes).

2) Data I need to compute CAC and LTV precisely
- Sales & marketing spend over the period you want to analyze (e.g., last quarter or last 12 months). Include both marketing and sales (and SDR/AE) costs.
- Number of new customers acquired in that same period.
- Definition of “customer” for you (new logos, new paid accounts, new paying ARR deals).
- Average revenue per customer (ARPC) or average contract value (ACV) and whether you want LTV on ARR or on a per-month basis. If you don’t have ARPC, give total revenue and active customer count so I can derive it.
- Gross margin (contribution margin) % on revenue (important for LTV).
- Churn rate (monthly or annual), or average customer lifetime in months/years. If you don’t track churn, let me know retention cohorts or we can model scenarios.

3) Formulas I’ll use (so you know what’s being calculated)
- CAC = (Total Sales & Marketing Spend in period) / (Number of New Customers in period)
- LTV (basic, subscription/SaaS approximation) = (ARPU_monthly * Gross_Margin%) / monthly_churn
  - Alternatively: LTV = ARPU_annual * Gross_Margin% / annual_churn
- LTV : CAC ratio = LTV / CAC
- CAC payback (months) = CAC / (ARPU_monthly * Gross_Margin%)

Benchmark guidance:
- Healthy LTV:CAC ~ 3x or greater (varies by stage).
- CAC payback ideally < 12 months for a growth-stage SaaS company; up to ~18–24 months may be acceptable for high-growth companies with investor support.

4) Illustrative worked examples (useful until you provide exact inputs)
I’ll show three scenarios with simple assumptions so you can see sensitivity:

Assumptions used in each example:
- Quarterly revenue = $3.2M → monthly revenue = $1.067M
- We’ll assume different ARPU, churn, and S&M spend scenarios.

Scenario A — conservative (higher churn, lower ARPU)
- ARPU (monthly per customer) = $5,000
- Gross margin = 70%
- Monthly churn = 2% (implied average customer lifetime ~50 months)
- S&M spend last quarter = $600,000
- New customers last quarter = 10

Calculations:
- CAC = 600,000 / 10 = $60,000
- LTV = (5,000 * 0.70) / 0.02 = 175,000
- LTV:CAC = 175,000 / 60,000 ≈ 2.9x
- CAC payback = 60,000 / (5,000 * 0.70) = 60,000 / 3,500 ≈ 17.1 months

Interpretation: LTV:CAC ~ 2.9x close to target but CAC payback is long (~17 months); with 20-month runway (if true) that’s borderline for accelerating growth.

Scenario B — base/likely
- ARPU monthly = $8,000
- Gross margin = 75%
- Monthly churn = 1.5%
- S&M spend quarter = $900,000
- New customers = 15

Calculations:
- CAC = 900,000 / 15 = $60,000
- LTV = (8,000 * 0.75) / 0.015 = 400,000
- LTV:CAC = 400,000 / 60,000 ≈ 6.7x
- CAC payback = 60,000 / (8,000 * 0.75) = 60,000 / 6,000 = 10 months

Interpretation: Very healthy LTV:CAC and acceptable payback.

Scenario C — aggressive/high ARPU, low churn
- ARPU monthly = $15,000
- Gross margin = 80%
- Monthly churn = 0.7%
- S&M spend quarter = $1.2M
- New customers = 20

Calculations:
- CAC = 1,200,000 / 20 = $60,000
- LTV = (15,000 * 0.80) / 0.007 = 1,714,286
- LTV:CAC ≈ 28.6x
- CAC payback = 60,000 / (15,000 * 0.80) = 60,000 / 12,000 = 5 months

Interpretation: Exceptional unit economics; very low payback and huge LTV. (This is possible for large enterprise deals with low churn.)

These examples show:
- CAC is very sensitive to number of new customers (denominator).
- LTV is very sensitive to churn and gross margin.
- Even the same CAC can be acceptable or problematic depending on ARPU/churn.

5) Suggested finance narrative structure for slides/doc
- Current position snapshot: cash, runway (explicit how calculated), monthly gross burn and net burn, last quarter revenue.
- Unit economics headline: CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC, CAC payback (with period and assumptions).
- Sensitivity chart: show LTV:CAC under 3-4 churn & ARPU scenarios.
- Operational levers: improve retention (reduce churn by X → LTV increases by Y), increase gross margin (pricing or cost-of-revenue), reduce CAC (optimize channels), accelerate payback (upsell/cross-sell).
- Ask/plan: if runway < required months for your growth plan, options: reduce burn, extend runway (cuts or hires), raise capital — show funding need with scenarios.
- Risks & mitigations: e.g., rising CAC, sales cycle length, customer concentration.

6) Next steps I can do for you
- If you send:
  - S&M spend (quarter or year)
  - # new customers in that period
  - Gross margin %
  - Churn rate (monthly or annual) or avg lifetime
  - Average revenue per customer (monthly or annual) or active customer count
I will compute exact CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC, CAC payback and produce a concise one-page narrative and bullets for your internal deck (I can also draft the slide text).

If you’d like, start by confirming the runway calculation (is net burn 105K/mo?) and give me S&M spend and new customers for the last quarter — I’ll run the exact numbers and produce the narrative.

