  - But you said burn = $240K/month — so either (a) the $240K is gross cash burn before revenue is applied and revenue offsets the rest (net burn = $105K), or (b) “revenue last quarter” is being reported on a different basis (e.g., annualized, or not recurring), or (c) one of the numbers is misstated.
- Also: revenue last quarter $3.2M → monthly revenue ≈ $1.0667M. If that revenue is recurring and cash receipts are roughly that, it would more than cover $240K burn (i.e., net cash flow positive), which contradicts having a positive runway constraint.

Please confirm:
1. Is $240K your gross cash burn (total cash outflows) or net burn (outflows minus operating revenue)?
2. Is the $3.2M last quarter recurring revenue (subscription/ARR style) or one-time / seasonal?
3. Can you provide (or estimate) these inputs for CAC/LTV calculations:
   - Total S&M (sales + marketing) spend in the period you want to use (quarter or year)
   - Number of new customers acquired in that same period
   - Total customers (active customers)
   - Gross margin % (or gross profit / revenue)
   - Churn rate (monthly or annual), or average customer lifetime
   - Any differences by customer cohort (enterprise vs SMB)

While you gather that, here’s a practical, repeatable approach plus examples so you can shape the finance narrative.

Key formulas (clean, copyable)
- Monthly revenue (Rm) = Quarterly revenue / 3
- ARPU (avg revenue per user) per month = Rm / Total_customers
- CAC = Total S&M spend (over period) / New_customers_acquired (in same period)
- Customer lifetime (months) ≈ 1 / monthly_churn_rate
- LTV = ARPU_month * Gross_Margin * Customer_lifetime_months
  (alternative: LTV = (ARPU_month * Gross_Margin) / monthly_churn)
- LTV:CAC ratio = LTV / CAC
- CAC payback (months) = CAC / (ARPU_month * Gross_Margin)

What to target (benchmarks, adjust by industry)
- LTV:CAC: >3 is healthy (SaaS/recurring), >4 is strong for high-growth enterprise; <3 needs scrutiny.
- CAC payback: <12 months for growth-stage companies often preferred; <18 months may be acceptable with high growth.
- Gross margin: SaaS typically 70–90%; product/physical goods lower.

Worked examples — using your revenue 3.2M/quarter (Rm ≈ $1,066,667). I’ll show three customer-mix scenarios to illustrate how LTV and CAC interplay.

Scenario A — Enterprise-heavy
- Total customers = 50 → ARPU_month = 1,066,667 / 50 = $21,333
- Gross margin = 80%; monthly churn = 0.5% → lifetime ≈ 200 months
- LTV = 21,333 * 0.80 * 200 = $3,413,334
- If CAC per new enterprise = $200,000 → LTV:CAC ≈ 17; payback ≈ 200k / (21,333*0.8) ≈ 11.7 months

Scenario B — Mid-market
- Total customers = 1,000 → ARPU_month = 1,066.67
- Gross margin = 75%; monthly churn = 1% → lifetime ≈ 100 months
- LTV = 1,066.67 * 0.75 * 100 = $80,000
- If CAC per new mid-market customer = $10,000 → LTV:CAC = 8; payback ≈ 10k / (1,066.67*0.75) ≈ 12.5 months

Scenario C — SMB / high-volume
- Total customers = 20,000 → ARPU_month = 53.33
- Gross margin = 60%; monthly churn = 3% → lifetime ≈ 33.3 months
- LTV = 53.33 * 0.60 * 33.3 ≈ $1,067
- If CAC per SMB = $200 → LTV:CAC ≈ 5.3; payback ≈ 200 / (53.33*0.6) ≈ 6.25 months
(If CAC = $500, LTV:CAC drops to ≈2.1 and payback ≈15.6 months — riskier)

Takeaways from examples
- LTV is extremely sensitive to churn and gross margin; small changes in churn have large effects on LTV.
- ARPU scale matters: fewer, larger customers → very large LTVs even with similar churn.
- CAC by channel matters: high CAC for enterprise is OK if LTV is huge; for SMB you must keep CAC low.

Suggested finance narrative bullets for your internal deck
- Present current unit economics clearly: ARPU, gross margin, churn, CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC, and CAC payback (months).
- Explain the runway calculation explicitly (cash / net_burn) and show what assumptions (growth investments, ramp in S&M, seasonality) produce your 20-month figure — because current headline numbers look inconsistent and investors/board will ask.
- Show sensitivity scenarios (best / base / stress) for:
