Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Quick note before we dive in: two of the numbers you gave conflict as stated.

- Cash = $2.1M
- Burn = $240K / month → implies runway = 2.1M / 240K ≈ 8.75 months
- You said runway = 20 months — that can only be true if you’re measuring runway using net burn (expenses minus cash receipts) or projecting revenue/cost changes that reduce net monthly burn to ≈$105K/month (2.1M/20).

Which is it? Are you quoting:
- “Gross” cash burn of $240K/month (cash outflows), and runway = cash / gross burn (≈8.8 months)?  
- Or runway after including expected/actual revenue and other inflows (net burn ≈$105K/month)?  

Tell me which interpretation is correct (or give current monthly revenue and monthly cash inflows), and I’ll use the right runway baseline in the narrative. Meanwhile I’ll explain CAC and LTV, show the calculations you’ll need, and give example scenarios and narrative language you can use.

Key formulas and definitions
- CAC (Customer Acquisition Cost) = Total Sales + Marketing spend in a period / New customers acquired in that period.
- ARPA (Average Revenue per Account) or ARPU = Revenue in period / Number of active customers in that period.
- Gross margin (%) = (Revenue − COGS) / Revenue.
- Monthly churn rate (for subscription) = % of customers lost per month.
- LTV (simple, for subscription businesses) = ARPU * Gross Margin % / Monthly churn rate.
  - Equivalent: LTV = Gross margin per month per customer * (1 / monthly churn).
- CAC payback (months) = CAC / (ARPU * Gross Margin %) — how many months to recoup CAC from gross margin.
- Healthy rule-of-thumb targets: LTV:CAC ≥ 3:1 and CAC payback ≤ 12 months (benchmarks vary by company stage and model).

What I need from you (or what we’ll assume)
To calculate CAC and LTV precisely, provide:
- Sales & marketing spend for the quarter (or last 12 months).
- Number of new customers acquired in that same period.
- Number of active paying customers (or starting/ending cohort counts).
- Gross margin (or COGS) — per sale or company-wide.
- Churn rate (monthly or quarterly) or average customer lifetime.
- Whether revenue $3.2M last quarter is recurring (ARR/quarterly subscription revenue), one-time, or mix.

If you don’t have those numbers, I’ll work with assumptions — here are two illustrative scenarios so your finance narrative can include sensitivity.

Scenario A — SaaS / mid-market example (illustrative)
Assumptions:
- Quarterly revenue = $3.2M → monthly revenue = $1.067M
- New customers last quarter = 80 (so average new ACV is high — adjust if you have a different count)
- Sales & Marketing spend last quarter = $600K
- Gross margin = 70%
- Monthly churn = 3%

Calculations:
- CAC = $600K / 80 = $7,500 per new customer.
- ARPU (monthly per customer average) — if total customers = 1,200 (example), monthly ARPU = 1,067,000 / 1,200 ≈ $889
- LTV = ARPU * Gross margin / churn = 889 * 0.70 / 0.03 ≈ $20,708
- LTV:CAC ≈ 20,708 / 7,500 ≈ 2.76x (close to 3x target)
- CAC payback = CAC / (ARPU * Gross margin) = 7,500 / (889 * 0.7) ≈ 12.1 months

Interpretation: CAC is borderline — payback around 12 months and LTV:CAC ~2.8. You’d either raise prices / increase retention (lower churn) or optimize channels to reduce CAC.

Scenario B — Enterprise / high ARPU example (illustrative)
Assumptions:
- Quarterly revenue = $3.2M
- New customers last quarter = 8 (enterprise deals)
- Sales & Marketing spend = $400K
- Gross margin = 60%
- Monthly churn = 1%

Calculations:
- CAC = $400K / 8 = $50,000
- If total customers = 200, monthly ARPU = 1,067,000 / 200 = $5,335
- LTV = 5,335 * 0.60 / 0.01 = $320,100
- LTV:CAC = 320,100 / 50,000 = 6.4x
- CAC payback = 50,000 / (5,335 * 0.6) ≈ 15.6 months

Interpretation: LTV:CAC looks excellent, but payback >12 months — typical for enterprise where you accept longer sales cycles if LTV is large. Might need bridge financing if payback is long.

Practical finance narrative (short bullets you can use)
- “We measure CAC as total sales & marketing spend divided by new customers in the quarter. For Qx, S&M spend was $X and we acquired Y new customers → CAC = $Z.”
- “We calculate LTV as ARPU × gross margin ÷ monthly churn. Using a gross margin of A% and churn of B%, our LTV is $C, giving an LTV:CAC of Dx and CAC payback of Emonths.”
- “Benchmark: target LTV:CAC >= 3. Current result of Dx suggests [we’re in-line / need improvement].”
- “Actions to improve unit economics: (1) reduce CAC by shifting spend to higher-converting channels, (2) raise ARPU via upsell/packaging, (3) reduce churn via onboarding and product enhancements, (4) tighten sales qualification to lower wasted spend.”
- “Runway note: current cash $2.1M. If gross burn is $240K/month, runway is ~8.8 months. If management reports 20 months runway, that implies net monthly burn of ~ $105K — verify whether runway uses net burn after revenue and one-offs. We should clearly state the runway calculation in any materials.”

What I can prepare for you (pick one)
- A short slide-ready finance narrative + one-page unit-economics table using your actual S&M spend, new customers, gross margin, churn. OR
- A sensitivity table (CAC, LTV, payback) under three scenarios (best / base / worst) showing impact on runway and funding needs. OR
- A draft email / deck text for leadership/investors explaining CAC/LTV and actions.

Next step
Send me these numbers (or tell me which of the example scenarios you want to use):
- Sales & marketing spend (quarter or last 12 months)
- New customers acquired in that period
- Total active customers (or ARPU)
- Gross margin %
- Monthly or quarterly churn (or average customer lifetime)
- Clarify how you define runway (gross burn vs net burn including revenue)

I’ll compute CAC, LTV, payback, LTV:CAC, produce a concise narrative paragraph and a one-page table you can paste into your materials.

