Thanks — I can help. Quick check first: there’s an inconsistency in the numbers you gave.

- You said cash = $2.1M and runway = 20 months, burn = $240K/month. But 2.1M / 240K = 8.75 months, not 20 months. Either the runway or the cash or the monthly burn is off. Can you confirm which is correct?

What I need from you to calculate CAC and LTV accurately
- Business model (SaaS, marketplace, e-commerce, one‑time sales, enterprise contracts, etc.)
- Period you want to measure (monthly, quarterly)
- Sales & Marketing spend for that period (or S&M % of revenue)
- Number of new customers acquired in that period
- Total active customers (or customers at period start and end)
- Revenue by period (you gave last quarter = $3.2M — that helps)
- Gross margin (or cost of goods sold %)
- Retention / churn (monthly or annual) or cohort retention curves
If you can’t provide all of those I can show scenarios and sensitivities.

Key formulas and how to use them
- Monthly revenue = quarterly revenue / 3 = $3.2M / 3 = $1,066,667
- ARR (simple annualized run‑rate) = monthly revenue × 12 = $12,800,000
- CAC = Total sales & marketing spend in period / Number of new customers acquired in that same period
- ARPU (monthly) = Monthly revenue / Total active customers
- Average customer lifetime (months) = 1 / monthly churn rate
- LTV (gross-margin adjusted) = ARPU × Gross margin % × Average customer lifetime
  (alternatively LTV = (ARPU × Gross margin %) / monthly churn)
- CAC payback (months) = CAC / (ARPU × Gross margin %)
- LTV:CAC = LTV / CAC — target benchmark: >3 is healthy for SaaS growth-stage companies; >5 is excellent; <3 suggests poor unit economics

Example scenarios (illustrative — change inputs as needed)
Assumptions common to all examples:
- Monthly revenue = $1,066,667 (from your quarter)
Example A — “Base” (SaaS, medium churn)
- S&M = 30% of revenue → monthly S&M = 0.30 × 1,066,667 = $320,000
- New customers per month = 100
→ CAC = 320,000 / 100 = $3,200
- Total active customers = 600 → ARPU = 1,066,667 / 600 = $1,777.78 / month
- Gross margin = 70%, monthly churn = 2% → Avg lifetime = 50 months
→ LTV = 1,777.78 × 0.70 × 50 = $62,222
→ CAC payback = 3,200 / (1,777.78 × 0.70) ≈ 2.6 months
→ LTV:CAC ≈ 19.4 (very high — likely means assumptions (customers, churn) need checking)

Example B — “Conservative” (higher churn, fewer customers)
- S&M same = $320,000; new customers = 50/month → CAC = 6,400
- Total customers = 400 → ARPU = 2,666.67
- Gross margin = 60%, churn = 4% → Avg lifetime = 25 months
→ LTV = 2,666.67 × 0.60 × 25 = $40,000
→ CAC payback = 6,400 / (2,666.67 × 0.60) ≈ 4.0 months
→ LTV:CAC ≈ 6.25

Example C — “Aggressive” (low ARPU, high churn)
- S&M = $320,000; new customers = 200/month → CAC = 1,600
- Total customers = 2,000 → ARPU = 533.33
- Gross margin = 50%, churn = 8% → Avg lifetime = 12.5 months
→ LTV = 533.33 × 0.50 × 12.5 = $3,333
→ CAC payback = 1,600 / (533.33 × 0.50) ≈ 6.0 months
→ LTV:CAC ≈ 2.08 (weak; indicates need to improve retention or reduce CAC)

What this shows
- CAC and LTV are extremely sensitive to churn, ARPU, and the number of new customers. Small changes in churn or customer counts change LTV dramatically.
- Useful investor / internal benchmarks:
  - LTV:CAC > 3 (healthy); >5 (very strong)
  - CAC payback < 12 months (growth-friendly); < 6 months (very efficient)
  - S&M as % of revenue varies by stage — early growth companies often spend 30–80%+ on S&M.

Suggested finance narrative structure for internal materials
1. One‑page summary: current cash, true runway (please confirm), monthly burn, ARR, and immediate ask/plan.
2. Unit economics snapshot:
