Thanks — I can help. First, one quick data inconsistency to clarify, then the formulas, the data I need from you, a few worked examples (so you can see sensitivity), target benchmarks you can cite in the finance narrative, and a suggested short narrative you can drop into internal materials.

1) Quick clarification (important)
- You said: cash $2.1M, runway 20 months, burn $240K/month, revenue last quarter $3.2M. Those three numbers don’t line up mathematically:
  - If runway = cash / net burn, 2.1M / 20 months = net burn ≈ $105K/month.
  - If net burn = $240K/month, runway = 2.1M / 240K ≈ 8.8 months.
  - Also revenue last quarter of $3.2M → average monthly revenue ≈ $1.067M. Are the burn and runway figures (a) pre-revenue, (b) excluding some revenue streams, or (c) referring to operating burn vs net cash burn? Please confirm how you’re computing runway and what “burn rate $240K” represents (gross cash outflow, net burn after revenue, or payroll-only, etc.).

2) CAC and LTV — formulas and required inputs
- CAC (customer acquisition cost):
  - CAC = (Sales & Marketing spend in period) / (Number of new customers acquired in that period).
  - Need: S&M spend (ad spend + sales salaries/commissions + agency fees, etc.) for the period and count of new customers acquired in same period.

- LTV (customer lifetime value), common formula (revenue-based, gross-margin adjusted):
  - LTV = (ARPU per period) × (Gross margin %) × (Average customer lifetime in periods)
  - If you use churn: Average customer lifetime (in months) = 1 / monthly churn rate ⇒ LTV = ARPU_month × GM% × (1 / churn_monthly)
  - Need: ARPU (average revenue per customer per month or year), gross margin (contribution margin after COGS), and churn/retention (or direct average lifetime).

3) Typical KPI targets (benchmarks to include in narrative)
- LTV : CAC ratio — target ≥ 3 : 1 (2 : 1 is minimum acceptable for some businesses; <1 is unsustainable).
- CAC payback period — target ≤ 12 months (faster payback better early-stage; enterprise SaaS can tolerate longer if LTV is high).
- S&M as % of revenue — varies by stage; 20–40% common for growth-stage SaaS; earlier stage often higher.
- Gross margin — for SaaS expect 70–90%; product businesses may be lower.
- Churn — lower is better; benchmark depends on B2B vs B2C and contract terms.

4) Worked examples (pick the one closest to your business)
Example A — B2B SaaS-style numbers:
- Assumptions: ARPU = $2,000/month, gross margin = 75%, monthly churn = 2% (0.02).
- LTV = 2,000 × 0.75 × (1 / 0.02) = 2,000 × 0.75 × 50 = $75,000
- If CAC = $25,000 → LTV:CAC = 3.0, payback period = CAC / (ARPU × GM%) = 25,000 / (2,000 × 0.75) ≈ 16.7 months (longer than desired — you’d want either lower CAC or higher ARPU).

Example B — lower ARPU, faster churn:
- Assumptions: ARPU = $200/month, GM = 70%, monthly churn = 5% (0.05).
- LTV = 200 × 0.70 × (1 / 0.05) = 200 × 0.7 × 20 = $2,800
- If CAC = $1,200 → LTV:CAC ≈ 2.33 (below the 3:1 ideal), payback = 1,200 / (200 × 0.7) ≈ 8.6 months (acceptable payback).

5) What I can compute for you (please provide these)
- Sales & Marketing spend for last quarter (or last 12 months).
- Number of new customers acquired in that same period (quarter or year).
- Total active customers (to get ARPU if you prefer).
- Gross margin (or COGS) and whether you want to use contribution margin.
- Monthly or annual churn (or average customer lifetime).
- Confirmation of how runway and burn are calculated (see point 1).

6) Suggested finance narrative (short, actionable)
You can use this template and I’ll adapt with real numbers:
- “Stellar Labs closed Q[ ] with $3.2M in revenue (≈ $1.07M/month). We currently hold $2.1M in cash. Our stated runway of 20 months assumes a net burn of ~$105K/month; please confirm this is net of operating revenue. To ensure long-term sustainability we’re focusing on two levers: lowering CAC and increasing LTV. Key targets: reach an LTV:CAC ≥ 3 and CAC payback ≤ 12 months. To get there we will (1) optimize our highest-performing acquisition channels to reduce CAC by X–Y%, (2) increase ARPU through packaging and upsells, and (3) reduce churn by improving onboarding and account management. I will produce scenarios showing how 10–30% improvements in CAC or churn translate to runway extension and profitability thresholds.”

7) Next steps / offer
- If you give me your S&M spend and new-customer counts (quarterly or annual), gross margin, and churn (or customer counts and total revenue so I can derive ARPU), I’ll:
  - calculate CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC, and CAC payback,
  - run sensitivity scenarios (e.g., what happens to runway and LTV:CAC if churn improves by 1–3 percentage points or CAC falls by 20%),
  - produce 1–2 short slides or a concise paragraph for your internal deck.

Which numbers can you share now? If you want, give the quarter S&M spend + new customers and churn, and I’ll compute the exact CAC/LTV and craft the finance narrative with concrete figures.

