Thanks — I can help. A few quick points up front, then (1) the formulas / data you need, (2) a few worked examples (scenarios) so you can see sensitivity, and (3) suggested finance narrative bullets you can drop into slides.

Immediate flag
- The numbers you gave contain an inconsistency: 2.1M cash / 240K burn = 8.75 months runway if 240K is your net burn. You said runway = 20 months — please confirm how you computed runway (e.g., is 240K gross burn and you expect revenue to reduce net burn, or do you have committed funding, cuts planned, or projected revenue baked in?). I’ll use your clarification below to show runway impact once we have CAC/LTV.

1) What I need from you (minimum inputs)
To compute CAC and LTV accurately I need:
- Sales & Marketing spend for the period you want to measure CAC (e.g., last quarter, last 12 months). Include both direct marketing and sales compensation/commissions (and agency fees).
- New customers acquired in that same period (use same cohort period).
- Whether your business is subscription (SaaS) or one-time-sales / e-commerce / enterprise (different LTV formula).
- Average Revenue Per User (ARPU) for the same cadence (monthly or quarterly) or average contract value (ACV).
- Gross margin on revenue (percent).
- Churn (monthly or annual) or retention curve (if subscription).
If you don’t have churn, I can work with cohort retention or use reasonable assumptions to model scenarios.

2) Key formulas (clean, copy-friendly)
- CAC = (Sales + Marketing spend in period) / (Number of new customers acquired in period)
- For subscription businesses:
  - Avg monthly gross profit per customer = ARPU_month * Gross_Margin
  - Average customer lifetime (months) ≈ 1 / monthly_churn_rate
  - LTV = Avg monthly gross profit per customer * Average lifetime
  - Payback period (months) = CAC / (Avg monthly gross profit per customer)
- For non-subscription (repeat purchase) businesses:
  - LTV = Avg gross profit per purchase * Expected # purchases per customer (or average lifetime purchases)
- Rule-of-thumb: target LTV:CAC > 3x and payback ideally < 12 months (earlier-stage B2B often aims < 12-18 months).

3) Worked examples / scenarios
I’ll base these on your revenue last quarter = $3.2M (monthly revenue ≈ $1.067M). I’ll also assume an S&M spend and typical SaaS-style gross margin as illustrative—please replace with your real inputs.

Assumptions used in examples (changeable):
- Sales & Marketing (quarter): 20% of revenue → $640k for the quarter
- New customers acquired that quarter: 200 (scenario-specific)
- ARPU per customer per month: $2,000
- Gross margin: 70%
- Monthly churn scenarios: 1%, 3%, 7%

Example calculations (plug-and-play)
- CAC = $640,000 / 200 = $3,200 per new customer
- Avg monthly gross profit per customer = $2,000 * 70% = $1,400

LTV and LTV:CAC under different churns:
- If monthly churn = 1%:
  - Lifetime ≈ 1 / 0.01 = 100 months
  - LTV ≈ $1,400 * 100 = $140,000
  - LTV:CAC ≈ 140,000 / 3,200 ≈ 43.8x
  - Payback months = 3,200 / 1,400 ≈ 2.3 months
- If monthly churn = 3%:
  - Lifetime ≈ 33.3 months → LTV ≈ $46,667 → LTV:CAC ≈ 14.6x → Payback ≈ 2.3 months
- If monthly churn = 7%:
  - Lifetime ≈ 14.3 months → LTV ≈ $20,000 → LTV:CAC ≈ 6.25x → Payback ≈ 2.3 months

Notes about these results:
- Payback months depend only on CAC and gross profit per month (so in these examples payback is ~2.3 months because CAC and monthly gross profit were fixed). LTV changes with churn.
- These example LTV:CAC ratios are very healthy — this is because assumed ARPU and margin are high relative to CAC. If ARPU or margin are lower, or CAC higher, ratios will drop quickly.

4) Quick sensitivity table you can run (recommended)
Vary any two of: ARPU, CAC or monthly churn. Example scenarios to display on a slide:
- High ARPU / low churn (enterprise): high LTV, large multiple
- Mid ARPU / mid churn (current baseline)
- Low ARPU / high churn (worst case)

5) How this ties to runway and the finance narrative
- Unit economics drive how aggressively you should spend on growth. If LTV:CAC >> 3 and payback is short, you can accelerate S&M and extend runway through revenue growth.
- Runway calculation: Runway months = Cash / Net burn. Net burn = Gross cash outflow (opex incl. S&M) − Cash inflow (revenue). If the $240K burn you quoted is your net burn then runway = 2.1M / 240K ≈ 8.8 months. If $240K is gross burn, and monthly revenue is ~$1.067M (from $3.2M last quarter) your net cash flow is positive and runway would be much longer — please confirm which is correct.
- Use CAC & payback to forecast how a marketing ramp will alter revenue growth and net burn. Example: if payback = 3 months and you double S&M for three months, revenue and net burn change quickly because those customers generate gross margin before payback completes.

6) Suggested slide / finance narrative bullets
- “We compute CAC as total S&M / new customers each period; LTV = (ARPU_month * gross_margin) / monthly_churn. Target LTV:CAC > 3 and payback < 12 months.”
- “Current baseline: [insert your real CAC], [insert LTV], LTV:CAC = [x]. Payback = [y] months.”
- “Sensitivity: at ±20% CAC and churn +/- 2% our LTV:CAC moves from [low] to [high].”
- “Implications: given current cash of $2.1M and reported burn, runway is [compute once you confirm net burn]. If we prioritize retention improvements (reduce monthly churn by 1–2%), LTV increases by [calc], enabling sustainable S&M scale with minimal additional capital.”
- “Action plan: 1) provide exact S&M and customer acquisition counts so we can compute true CAC and payback; 2) run cohort LTV and retention analysis to validate LTV; 3) model fundraising needs under three acquisition spend scenarios.”

7) Next steps I can do for you
- If you give me: S&M spend (last quarter or last 12 months), new customers acquired in that period, ARPU (or # customers), gross margin, and churn — I’ll compute exact CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC, payback months and produce a 3-scenario sensitivity table (and the updated runway impact).
- I can also draft the finance slide text and 1–2 charts (LTV:CAC sensitivity and payback months vs CAC) for your internal materials.

Tell me which period to use (quarter or trailing 12 months), and paste the S&M spend, new customers, ARPU (or total customers), gross margin, churn, and confirm how the 20-month runway was derived. I’ll run the numbers and produce slide-ready statements.

