Good — I can help. First, a quick note: some of the numbers you gave don’t align (I’ll show how and what I need). After that I’ll give formulas, worked examples (so you can plug in your real inputs), a suggested finance narrative for internal materials, and recommended next steps.

1) Quick consistency check
- You said: cash = $2.1M, burn = $240K/month, runway = 20 months, revenue last quarter = $3.2M.
- If runway is simply cash / burn then 2.1M / 240K = 8.75 months (not 20 months).
- If runway = cash / net burn and runway = 20 months, net burn must be 2.1M / 20 = $105K/month. That implies revenue offsets $240K − $105K = $135K/month. But quarterly revenue $3.2M ⇒ monthly revenue ≈ $1.067M — so those figures are inconsistent.

Please confirm which numbers are correct (or whether “burn” you gave is gross operating spend and revenue offsets it). I’ll proceed with CAC/LTV guidance assuming you’ll give me the missing inputs or pick the scenario you want modeled.

2) What I need from you to compute CAC and LTV precisely
- Period for analysis (monthly/quarterly/annual)
- Sales & Marketing spend in the period (total S&M $)
- Number of new customers acquired in the same period
- Total customers or active customer count (to compute ARPA)
- Churn rate (monthly or annual) or retention by cohort
- Gross margin (contribution margin; e.g., 70–85% for software, lower for physical products)
- Any upsell/expansion rate (optional)

If you provide those I’ll compute CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC, and CAC payback precisely.

3) Formulas (quick reference)
- CAC = (Total S&M spend in period) / (New customers acquired in that period)
- ARPA (monthly) = Monthly revenue / Number of customers
- Customer lifetime (months) ≈ 1 / monthly churn rate
- LTV = ARPA * gross_margin * customer_lifetime (same-period units)
- LTV:CAC = LTV / CAC
- CAC payback (months) = CAC / (ARPA * gross_margin)

4) Worked examples using your revenue (quarter = $3.2M → monthly ≈ $1,066,667)
I’ll show scenarios for different customer counts, churn rates and gross margin (useful for sensitivity analysis).

Assumptions common to all examples below: monthly revenue = $1,066,667.

Example A — mid-sized customer base (500 customers)
- ARPA = 1,066,667 / 500 = $2,133 / month
- Gross margin = 70% (example)
- Monthly churn scenarios:
  - 2% churn → lifetime = 50 months → LTV = 2,133 * 0.70 * 50 = $74,667
  - 3% churn → lifetime ≈ 33.3 months → LTV ≈ $49,758
  - 5% churn → lifetime = 20 months → LTV ≈ $29,867

Interpretation: If your CAC per new customer is $20,000:
- LTV:CAC at 3% churn = 49,758 / 20,000 = 2.49x (below ideal 3x)
- CAC payback = 20,000 / (2,133 * 0.70) ≈ 13.4 months (slower than 12-month target)

Example B — larger customer base (1,000 customers)
- ARPA = 1,066,667 / 1,000 = $1,067 / month
- GM = 70%
- 3% churn → lifetime 33.3 months → LTV = 1,067 * 0.7 * 33.3 ≈ $24,879
- If CAC = $10,000 → LTV:CAC = 2.49x again (same ratio since ARPA halves and CAC assumed halves)

Example C — fewer customers (200 customers)
- ARPA = 1,066,667 / 200 = $5,333 / month
- GM = 70%
- 3% churn → LTV ≈ 5,333 * 0.7 * 33.3 ≈ $124,398

These examples show LTV scales with ARPA and with lower churn; CAC/CAC payback depends on S&M efficiency.

5) Benchmarks and what investors / leadership look for
- LTV:CAC target: ≥ 3x is healthy (some say 3–5x for SaaS). Below 3x warrants either lower CAC or higher margins/retention.
- CAC payback: ideal < 12 months (for fast-growth businesses 12–18 months can be acceptable; for capital-efficient models shorter is better).
- Gross margin: high GM (70–90%) improves payback and LTV.
- Churn: small changes in churn have large impact on LTV. Reducing churn from 5% → 3% can double LTV.
- Cohort analysis: show LTV and CAC by cohort (cohort growth and retention trends matter more than one-off averages).

6) Suggested finance narrative (short version you can drop into internal materials)
- “Our current quarterly revenue is $3.2M (≈$1.07M/month). To assess unit economics we track CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC and CAC payback. LTV is driven primarily by ARPA and retention; improving gross margin and reducing churn materially increases LTV.”
- “We define CAC as total S&M spend per quarter divided by new customers acquired in that quarter. Our target LTV:CAC is >3x and CAC payback <12 months. If either metric slips we will prioritize (a) reducing CAC through more efficient channels; (b) improving retention via product/ops investment; or (c) expanding ARPA through upsell.”
- “Current cash of $2.1M gives us runway of X months. Note: reconcile reported burn ($240K/month) with revenue generation ($1.07M/month) — we should report net burn (expenses − revenue) for runway calculations.”
- “Action plan: finalize accurate S&M spend and new customer counts for last 3 quarters, compute cohort LTVs, and present LTV:CAC by cohort and CAC payback. Use sensitivity table for churn and gross margin to guide spend and product investment decisions.”

7) Recommended next steps (what I can do now)
- If you give me: total S&M spend last quarter (or month), new customers added in that period, total active customers, gross margin and churn, I’ll compute:
  - CAC, ARPA, customer lifetime, LTV, LTV:CAC, CAC payback
