Thanks — I can help. Quick note before we dive in: the numbers you gave are internally inconsistent and I need you to confirm which is correct.

- You wrote cash = $2.1M and runway = 20 months, but also burn = $240K/month.
  - If net burn = $240K/month then runway = 2.1M / 240K ≈ 8.8 months (not 20).
  - If runway = 20 months then implied net burn = 2.1M / 20 = $105K/month (not 240K).
Which of those three numbers (cash, runway, burn) is wrong / how are you defining “burn” (gross vs net)?

What I can do immediately
- Give you the exact formulas and a short framework for CAC and LTV.
- Show 3 short example scenarios (enterprise / midmarket / SMB) using reasonable assumptions so you can see how customer mix drives allowable CAC, LTV:CAC and payback.
- Tell you what exact inputs I need to compute your real CAC and LTV and draft the finance narrative for the deck.

Key formulas (use these in any slide)
- CAC = Total Sales & Marketing spend over period / Number of new customers acquired in that period
- LTV (subscription/SaaS standard) = (Average Revenue per Account per year × Gross margin %) / Annual churn rate
  - Alternative (non-subscription / e-commerce): LTV = Avg order value × # orders per year × gross margin × expected lifetime (yrs)
- LTV:CAC = LTV / CAC (target commonly ≥ 3:1)
- Payback months = CAC / (monthly gross contribution = (ARPA/12) × gross margin)

What I need from you to compute exact CAC & LTV
- Time window (last 12 months? last quarter?)
- Total S&M (sales + marketing) spend in that period (cash basis)
- Number of new customers acquired in that period
- Total revenue in that period (we have quarterly $3.2M — good)
- Average Revenue per Account (ARR or ARPA) or number of customers (so I can derive ARPA)
- Gross margin (or COGS) %
- Churn (annual churn rate or monthly churn)
- Whether revenue is subscription/renewal-based or transactional (changes LTV calc)

Quick example scenarios (annualize your quarter: revenue = $3.2M → annual = $12.8M; assume gross margin = 75%)
Scenario A — Enterprise (ARPA = $100k/year)
- Customers = 12.8M / 100k = 128
- Assume churn = 10% → LTV = 100k × 0.75 / 0.10 = $750k
- “Rule-of-thumb” allowable CAC at 3:1 = $250k
- Monthly gross contribution per customer = (100k/12)×0.75 = $6,250 → payback for CAC=$250k is 40 months (too long)
- To hit 12-month payback CAC must be ≈ $75k → LTV:CAC = 10:1 (very healthy)

Scenario B — Midmarket (ARPA = $25k/year)
- Customers = 12.8M / 25k = 512
- Assume churn = 20% → LTV = 25k × 0.75 / 0.20 = $93.75k
- Allowable CAC at 3:1 = $31.25k
- Monthly contribution = (25k/12)×0.75 = $1,562.50 → payback for CAC=$31.25k is ~20 months
- To get 12-month payback CAC ≈ $18.75k → LTV:CAC ≈ 5:1

Scenario C — SMB (ARPA = $5k/year)
- Customers = 12.8M / 5k = 2,560
- Assume churn = 30% → LTV = 5k × 0.75 / 0.30 = $12.5k
- Allowable CAC at 3:1 = $4.17k
- Monthly contribution = (5k/12)×0.75 = $312.50 → payback for CAC=$4.17k is ~13.3 months
- To meet 12-month payback CAC ≈ $3.75k → LTV:CAC ≈ 3.33:1

How to use this in the finance narrative (slides / talking points)
- Slide: headline metrics — ARR (annualized revenue), cash, burn (explicitly state whether gross or net), runway (show math).
- Slide: unit economics summary — ARPA, gross margin, churn, LTV, CAC, LTV:CAC, payback months. Show target benchmarks (LTV:CAC ≥ 3, payback ≤ 12 months, gross margin > 60–70%).
- Slide: sensitivity / scenarios — show how LTV:CAC and payback change with ARPA, churn, and S&M spend mix (enterprise vs SMB).
- Slide: actions & asks — if CAC/payback are unfavorable, propose: reduce CAC (more efficient channels), raise prices or increase ARPA (upsells), reduce churn (product/CS investments), improve gross margin, or raise-money contingency (show amount required to reach milestone X).
- Slide: fundraising ask (if applicable) — show runway under base case vs improved unit-economics case; tie dollars requested to expected improvement (e.g., spend $X on S&M with expected CAC payback Y months and Y new customers → incremental ARR).

Recommended targets and red flags
- Targets: LTV:CAC ≥ 3, payback ≤ 12 months (growth stage may tolerate longer but we should present the trade-offs).
- Red flags: payback > 18 months, LTV:CAC < 2, churn > 25% without clear plan.

Next steps
- Confirm which of your cash/runway/burn numbers are correct.
- Share the inputs listed above (S&M spend, new customers, gross margin, churn, customer counts or ARPA).
Once you share those, I’ll:
- Calculate your actual CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC, payback months.
- Produce a concise finance narrative and a one-page metrics table you can drop into the deck (and I can draft slide wording).

Would you like me to:
A) Run these calculations now based on assumed values and produce a ready paragraph you can paste into a deck, or
B) Wait until you confirm the runway/burn and send the S&M and customer numbers so I compute exact metrics?

